UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 1: Numbers in the real world', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA packet label shows 1,000 mL. Is that an exact measure for the recipe needing 1,000 mL, or only a rough idea? Why?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTreat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAssuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind or invent six real-world numbers (scores, prices in whole dollars, distances in whole km, crowd signs). For each, say exact or estimate, name the places you notice and one decision the number supports.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-RN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find or invent six real-world numbers (scores, prices in whole dollars, distances in whole km, crowd signs). For each, say exact or estimate, name the places you notice and one decision the number supports. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: A crowd sign says 2,500. That is likely a rounded or planned figure, not a guarantee every person was counted. For ordering chairs we may need a more exact count.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions. Correction: Treat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough.","Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the working\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value.","Skip the working because the first guess looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers.","Skip the working because the first guess looked neat.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new problem\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Treat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["Treat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount.","Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A packet label shows 1,000 mL. Is that an exact measure for the recipe needing 1\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["The most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Write the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the met\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Treat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["Treat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount.","Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["The new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Solve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for yo\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant working with accurate reasoning and named places or values.","question":"Solve a fresh problem that uses the central skill. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: A crowd sign says 2,500. That is likely a rounded or planned figure, not a guarantee every person was counted. For ordering chairs we may need a more exact count.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change value or method, not just make the working longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected working and a method-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely calculation or place-value error, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions. Correction: Treat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise real world.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use real world with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify estimate and explain why it matters.","I can practise exact independently on a new prompt.","I can notice context needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA crowd sign says 2,500. That is likely a rounded or planned figure, not a guarantee every person was counted. For ordering chairs we may need a more exact count.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA packet label shows 1,000 mL. Is that an exact measure for the recipe needing 1,000 mL, or only a rough idea? Why?\n\nFEEDBACK\nTreat it as the stated exact pack size for the recipe unless measuring shows otherwise; labels claim a specific amount.","explain":"Numbers in the real world appear on signs, scores and measures. Decide when an exact count is needed and when a place-value estimate is enough.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Assuming every displayed number is approximate (or every one is exact) without context causes poor decisions. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Real-world number that may be an estimate.","caption":"Ask whether exactness is required.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Crowd sign</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2,500</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Likely</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">rounded or planned figure</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A crowd sign says 2,500. That is likely a rounded or planned figure, not a guarantee every person was counted. For order Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A crowd sign says 2,500. That is likely a rounded or planned figure, not a guarantee every person was counted. For order Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA crowd sign says 2,500. That is likely a rounded or planned figure, not a guarantee every person was counted. For ordering chairs we may need a more exact count. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Pack label used as a stated measure.","caption":"Context decides exact versus estimate.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b8744